---
// "How We Pair Statements" (INC-11, brief §10 / D9). States the bright line for
// pairing a statement to a vote, and that the site draws no conclusion from a pair.
// Linked from every member timeline. UI-chrome copy — strictly neutral, no
// relational language about any member.
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
---
<Layout title="How statements and votes are paired — Civic Ledger">
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How statements and votes are paired</h1>

  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-neutral-700">
    Some pages place a public statement next to a recorded vote. This page explains
    the exact, mechanical rule for when that happens — and, just as importantly, what
    the pairing does and does not claim.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">The rule</h2>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-neutral-700">
    A statement is paired to a measure only by the measure's <strong>bill
    identifier</strong> (for example <code>hr2-119</code>). A statement is shown next
    to a vote when, and only when, a bill it explicitly names is the bill that vote was
    on. There is no fuzzy text matching and no interpretation of what a statement was
    "really about."
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-neutral-700">
    The single exception is a formal short title. Because a statement often names a
    measure by its title rather than its number, a title may be paired — but only when
    the site's maintainer has explicitly recorded, in a public allowlist, that this
    exact title corresponds to that exact bill. Nothing is matched by resemblance.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">
    What a pairing means
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-neutral-700">
    A pairing places two facts side by side: a statement the member made, quoted
    verbatim with a link to its source and a permanent archived copy; and a vote the
    member cast, with the official question and record. That is all it is —
    <strong>adjacency, not a conclusion</strong>. The site does not assert that the two
    agree or disagree, that one caused the other, or that they occurred in any
    particular order. Readers see the record and draw their own conclusions.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">
    Which statements appear
  </h2>
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-neutral-700">
    Every statement is selected and entered by a person, quoted verbatim, and stored
    only after a permanent public archive of its source has been captured and
    verified. If that archived copy ever stops resolving, the statement is withheld
    until it can be re-captured. Statements are never generated, paraphrased, or
    summarized by an automated process.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-8 text-xs text-neutral-500">
    See also the <a href="/methods" class="underline hover:text-neutral-700">methodology</a>.
  </p>
</Layout>
